  W.cultureCustoms = "【Cigars to celebrate a birth】The custom of a new father handing out cigars to friends and colleagues and lighting up together to celebrate a child's birth is one of the signature cigar traditions of the English-speaking world. Some point to the influence of indigenous gift-exchange customs, but the direct form is generally traced to Victorian Britain in the nineteenth century, said to derive from fathers — unable to be present at the birth — sharing a smoke with companions waiting in another room to toast the good news.\n【It's a Boy! / It's a Girl!】The colour-coding of celebratory cigars — a blue band for a boy, pink for a girl — is held to be a comparatively recent custom, spread as a promotion by an American cigar brand in the 1950s. Beyond births, the culture of marking life's milestones — weddings, promotions — with a cigar has been handed down to the present day.\n【A symbol of success and power】The cigar has historically stood for wealth, success and authority, beloved of political and business grandees from Churchill to Kennedy. The culture of exchanging cigars to seal a deal or at celebratory gatherings, and the standing of cigars in China as prized luxury gifts and collectables, lie on the same continuum.\n【The lector (the factory reader)】In Cuba, around 1865, the custom arose of placing a reader in the factory to relieve the monotony of the rollers' work. It is said to have begun with readings at the El Fígaro factory in Havana and the workers' journal 'La Aurora'; the artisans chose the books by vote, and showed their thanks by rapping their chavetas (rolling knives) on the workbenches. Though there were periods when the practice took on political colouring and was suppressed, the tradition lives on today.\n【Brand names born of literature】Novels read aloud in the factories became a source of brand names. Montecristo, after Dumas's 'The Count of Monte Cristo', was founded by Menéndez in 1935. Romeo y Julieta, established in 1875, is said to have been made famous by the factory head Pepín Rodríguez, a devotee of Shakespeare's play. Many marques take their names from literature and history.\n【The origin of the cigar band】The modern band is said to originate with the Dutch-born Gustave Bock, who in the 1830s wrapped his own signed paper bands around cigars for export as a guarantee of quality and authenticity. By the 1850s many Cuban manufacturers had adopted the practice. In Britain an etiquette arose of removing the band so as not to flaunt the marque in company, while in America keeping or removing it is regarded as a matter of personal taste.\n【Cutting and lighting】The basic cut removes only the cap, cleanly and in a single stroke, just above the shoulder. Punch and V-cuts are also used. To light, hold the flame an inch or two away and toast the whole foot evenly while rotating the cigar. Use a lighter, matches or a cedar spill, and never bring the naked flame of a gas lighter — which can taint the flavour — too close.\n【The etiquette of ash and extinguishing】Rather than tapping constantly, let the ash grow to an inch or two, then ease it off by rolling the cigar on the rim of the ashtray. Knocking it hard damages the cherry, the burning tip. When you have finished, the done thing is to rest the cigar on the edge of the ashtray and let it go out by itself; crushing it out like a cigarette is considered poor form, and causes a foul smell besides.\n【The box press】A cigar shaped square rather than round, pressed into form in a mould after rolling and before ageing. One theory holds that the shape arose naturally when cigars packed into boxes flattened in transit in the age of sail. Being densely packed, it is said to burn slowly and cool, with a more even, mellower flavour.\n【The father of the humidor】The idea of the modern humidor that holds humidity constant, and of the climate-controlled storage room, is credited to Zino Davidoff, who learned his trade in Cuba. He installed in his Geneva shop what is held to be the world's first air-conditioned storage room, establishing the philosophy of offering cigars in their best condition, and laid the foundations of today's storage culture.\n【Pairing and sociability】The habit of savouring a cigar slowly after dinner with coffee, brandy, whisky or rum has long been rooted in Europe, in Spain above all. In Japan and the Middle East it has grown into a bar culture of quietly enjoying pairings with spirits, and the cigar is cherished as a luxury that graces conversation and company.\n【The torcedor's craft】In Cuba the roller is called a 'torcedor', a trade demanding great skill in rolling up filler, binder and wrapper by hand. A master finishes more than a hundred cigars a day, and skill is assessed by grade. The gesture of cutting the wrapper with the special knife called a chaveta is the emblem of the craft, carrying the pride and cultural identity of the growing regions.\n【The meaning of 'puro'】The Spanish word for a cigar, 'puro', properly means 'pure', and is also used for a cigar made solely from the leaf of a single country. A cigar styled a 'puro', built from the leaf of one origin, declares the maker's pride in putting that region's character to the fore. The 'Nicaraguan puro' is a leading example.\n【The culture of ageing】Cigars are held to lose their rough edges and knit together in flavour when rested in a humidor after purchase, and there is a culture of treasuring vintage sticks aged over a span of years. The famous bars of Ginza and Hong Kong that make a speciality of long-aged Cuban stock are an extension of this, and collecting and ageing form one of the core pleasures of the devotee.\n【The cigar boom of the 1990s】Symbolised by 'Cigar Aficionado', launched in 1992, the 1990s saw the cigar catch fire in the United States as a status symbol of wealth and success. Celebrities graced the covers, and prices and demand soared. The boom lifted demand worldwide, helping propel the rise of Dominican and Nicaraguan production and the flowering of cigar-bar culture in city after city.\n【Charity humidor auctions】At the gala that crowns a producing country's festival, it is the custom for lavish bespoke humidors by celebrated artists to go under the hammer, the proceeds donated to good causes. At the Habanos Festival the winning bids are given to Cuba's public health system, and in 2025 the auction as a whole raised about €16.41 million (the top single lot being the Cohiba Behike 15th-anniversary humidor at about €4.6 million). It is the flower of a cigar culture that joins festivity to public benefit.\n【Smoking bans and smoking spaces】The spread of indoor smoking laws across the world has left its mark on cigar culture too. Spain, traditionally the largest market, brought a strict smoking ban covering bars and restaurants into force in 2011, restricting smoking in public spaces. Against this background, cigar lounges, bars and members' clubs equipped with dedicated humidors and ventilation have grown ever more important worldwide as legitimate spaces where devotees can gather and enjoy a cigar.\n【Gifts and collecting】The cigar has long been a gift expressing respect and congratulation, and in emerging markets — China above all — limited editions are treasured as important gifts and objects of investment. Rare vintages and commemorative releases change hands at high prices among collectors, and a mature collecting culture has grown up around keeping and ageing them in dedicated humidors.";
